      "content": "<h3>What is a TreeSet?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>TreeSet</strong> is a set that keeps its elements <strong>sorted at all times</strong>. Add forts in any order and they come out alphabetically; add numbers and they come out from small to large. It also never holds duplicates, and it can answer questions such as \"which fort comes right after Raigad?\" or \"give me all forts between P and S\".</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Package <code>java.util</code>. Implements <code>NavigableSet</code> (which extends <code>SortedSet</code>, which extends <code>Set</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Sorted order:</strong> by the elements' <strong>natural order</strong> (<code>Comparable</code>) or by a <code>Comparator</code> you supply.</li>\n<li><strong>No duplicates:</strong> \"duplicate\" means <code>compareTo</code> / <code>compare</code> returns 0, <em>not</em> <code>equals()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Slower than HashSet:</strong> add, remove and contains are <strong>O(log n)</strong> because it is a balanced tree.</li>\n<li><strong>No null</strong> with natural ordering (it cannot compare null). <strong>Not thread-safe.</strong></li>\n<li>Elements must be mutually comparable, otherwise <code>ClassCastException</code>.</li>\n<li>Use it when you need <strong>sorted output</strong> or <strong>range and neighbour queries</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"
