    {
      "content": "<h3>Reverse order and iteration</h3>\n<p>Walk the set from the end, or look at it upside down.</p>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>7. descendingSet(), descendingIterator() and reversed()</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>NavigableSet<E> descendingSet();  Iterator<E> descendingIterator();  NavigableSet<E> reversed()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> a reversed view / iterator</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(1)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> <code>descendingSet()</code> shows the same elements from largest to smallest, as a live view. <code>descendingIterator()</code> walks them that way. In Java 21 <code>reversed()</code> does the same as <code>descendingSet()</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Views are free (no copying). Because it is a <code>NavigableSet</code> itself, you can chain operations, for example <code>set.descendingSet().headSet(x)</code>.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet descendingSet(): the set in reverse order\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetDescendingSetDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> numbers = new TreeSet<>();\n        numbers.add(10);\n        numbers.add(20);\n        numbers.add(30);\n        numbers.add(40);\n\n        System.out.println(numbers.descendingSet());\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// [40, 30, 20, 10]",
        "// TreeSet descendingIterator(): loop from big to small\nimport java.util.Iterator;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetDescendingIteratorDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> numbers = new TreeSet<>();\n        numbers.add(10);\n        numbers.add(20);\n        numbers.add(30);\n        numbers.add(40);\n\n        Iterator<Integer> it = numbers.descendingIterator();\n        while (it.hasNext()) {\n            System.out.println(it.next());\n        }\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// 40\n// 30\n// 20\n// 10",
        "// TreeSet reversed() (Java 21): the set in reverse order\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetReversedDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> numbers = new TreeSet<>();\n        numbers.add(10);\n        numbers.add(20);\n        numbers.add(30);\n        numbers.add(40);\n\n        System.out.println(numbers.reversed());\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// [40, 30, 20, 10]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>8. iterator, forEach, stream and comparator()</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>Iterator<E> iterator();  void forEach(Consumer);  Stream<E> stream();  Comparator<? super E> comparator()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> elements in sorted order / the ordering in use</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Iteration, <code>forEach</code> and streams all visit the elements in <strong>sorted order</strong>. <code>comparator()</code> returns the Comparator that defines the order, or <code>null</code> when natural ordering is used.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Streams from a TreeSet are <code>SORTED</code> and <code>DISTINCT</code>, so <code>stream().sorted()</code> is free and <code>distinct()</code> is redundant. The iterator is fail-fast.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet iteration is sorted; a Comparator changes the order\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetOrderDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<String> cities = new TreeSet<>();\n        cities.add(\"Pune\");\n        cities.add(\"Agra\");\n        for (String c : cities) {\n            System.out.println(c);                     // Agra, Pune (A to Z)\n        }\n\n        TreeSet<String> reverse = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());   // Z to A\n        reverse.add(\"Pune\");\n        reverse.add(\"Agra\");\n        System.out.println(reverse);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Agra\n// Pune\n// [Pune, Agra]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Master program: every TreeSet method in one place</h3>\n<p>One single program that uses all the TreeSet methods you learned above, step by step. Read the numbered comments from top to bottom, run it, and match each output line with its step.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// MASTER PROGRAM: all important TreeSet methods in one program\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetMasterDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // 1. Create an empty TreeSet (always sorted)\n        TreeSet<Integer> numbers = new TreeSet<>();\n\n        // 2. add and addAll: sorted, duplicates ignored\n        numbers.add(30);\n        numbers.add(10);\n        numbers.add(30);                               // ignored\n        numbers.addAll(Set.of(20, 40, 50));\n        System.out.println(\"2. After adds    : \" + numbers);\n\n        // 3. size, isEmpty, contains\n        System.out.println(\"3. size/contains : \" + numbers.size() + \" / \" + numbers.contains(20));\n\n        // 4. first and last\n        System.out.println(\"4. first / last  : \" + numbers.first() + \" / \" + numbers.last());\n\n        // 5. Nearest values: lower, floor, ceiling, higher\n        System.out.println(\"5. lower(30)     : \" + numbers.lower(30));\n        System.out.println(\"   floor(30)     : \" + numbers.floor(30));\n        System.out.println(\"   ceiling(35)   : \" + numbers.ceiling(35));\n        System.out.println(\"   higher(50)    : \" + numbers.higher(50));\n\n        // 6. Range views: headSet, tailSet, subSet\n        System.out.println(\"6. headSet(30)   : \" + numbers.headSet(30));\n        System.out.println(\"   tailSet(30)   : \" + numbers.tailSet(30));\n        System.out.println(\"   subSet(20, 40): \" + numbers.subSet(20, 40));\n\n        // 7. Reverse order: descendingSet, reversed\n        System.out.println(\"7. descending    : \" + numbers.descendingSet());\n        System.out.println(\"   reversed      : \" + numbers.reversed());\n\n        // 8. Remove: remove, pollFirst, pollLast\n        numbers.remove(30);\n        System.out.println(\"8. pollFirst     : \" + numbers.pollFirst());\n        System.out.println(\"   pollLast      : \" + numbers.pollLast());\n        System.out.println(\"   Left          : \" + numbers);\n\n        // 9. A custom order with a Comparator\n        TreeSet<String> cities = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());\n        cities.add(\"Pune\");\n        cities.add(\"Agra\");\n        System.out.println(\"9. Z to A        : \" + cities);\n\n        // 10. clear\n        numbers.clear();\n        System.out.println(\"10. After clear  : \" + numbers);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// 2. After adds    : [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\n// 3. size/contains : 5 / true\n// 4. first / last  : 10 / 50\n// 5. lower(30)     : 20\n//    floor(30)     : 30\n//    ceiling(35)   : 40\n//    higher(50)    : null\n// 6. headSet(30)   : [10, 20]\n//    tailSet(30)   : [30, 40, 50]\n//    subSet(20, 40): [20, 30]\n// 7. descending    : [50, 40, 30, 20, 10]\n//    reversed      : [50, 40, 30, 20, 10]\n// 8. pollFirst     : 10\n//    pollLast      : 50\n//    Left          : [20, 40]\n// 9. Z to A        : [Pune, Agra]\n// 10. After clear  : []"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Module: Sorting methods</h3>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> a TreeSet <em>is</em> the sorting tool. You choose the rule: natural order (A to Z, small to big) or your own <code>Comparator</code>. The cards show each way, with a tiny program.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> the order is decided once, at construction, and cannot be changed later. Inserting is O(log n), so building a TreeSet of n elements is O(n log n), the same as one sort.</p>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>9. Natural order: no extra code</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> elements ordered by <code>compareTo</code></li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n) per add</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Numbers sort small to big and Strings sort alphabetically (capital letters before small letters). Just add the elements.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> The element type must implement <code>Comparable</code>. Your own class needs a <code>compareTo</code> method.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet natural order\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetSortNaturalDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>();\n        heights.add(1403);\n        heights.add(1312);\n        heights.add(1374);\n        heights.add(1356);\n\n        System.out.println(heights);   // always small to big\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// [1312, 1356, 1374, 1403]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>10. Reverse order: Comparator.reverseOrder()</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(Comparator.reverseOrder())</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> elements biggest first</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n) per add</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Hand the TreeSet a rule when you create it. <code>reverseOrder()</code> flips the natural order.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> The comparator is part of the set for its whole life, and it decides what a duplicate is. Pass it in the constructor, you cannot add it later.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet with a reverse comparator\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetSortReverseDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());\n        heights.add(1403);\n        heights.add(1312);\n        heights.add(1374);\n\n        System.out.println(heights);   // biggest first\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// [1403, 1374, 1312]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>11. Several rules: Comparator.comparing(...).thenComparing(...)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(Comparator)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> objects ordered by more than one field</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n) per add</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Sort forts by height (tallest first) and, when heights are equal, by name. Always finish with a field that makes every element different, otherwise equal-ranked elements are lost as \"duplicates\".</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> A <code>record</code> is ideal as the element: immutable, with <code>equals</code> and <code>hashCode</code> for free.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet ordered by height then name\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetSortMultiFieldDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        record Fort(String name, int height) { }\n\n        Comparator<Fort> order = Comparator.comparing(Fort::height, Comparator.reverseOrder())   // tallest first\n                .thenComparing(Fort::name);                                                       // tie-breaker\n\n        TreeSet<Fort> forts = new TreeSet<>(order);\n        forts.add(new Fort(\"Torna\", 1403));\n        forts.add(new Fort(\"Raigad\", 1356));\n        forts.add(new Fort(\"Rajgad\", 1356));   // same height as Raigad: the name keeps it as a separate element\n\n        forts.forEach(f -> System.out.println(f.name() + \" \" + f.height()));\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Torna 1403\n// Raigad 1356\n// Rajgad 1356"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>12. Ignore letter case: String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> strings sorted ignoring case</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n) per add</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> \"raigad\" and \"Raigad\" are different Strings, but with this comparator they are the <em>same</em> element, so the second one is ignored.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> This shows the rule: a TreeSet decides \"duplicate\" with the comparator, not with <code>equals</code>. That can be a feature (case-insensitive sets) or a bug (a comparator that ignores a field).</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet ignoring case\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetSortCaseDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<String> forts = new TreeSet<>(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER);\n        forts.add(\"raigad\");\n        forts.add(\"Raigad\");    // same as raigad for this comparator: ignored\n        forts.add(\"Torna\");\n        forts.add(\"sinhagad\");\n\n        System.out.println(forts);   // the first spelling of each stays\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// [raigad, sinhagad, Torna]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>13. Reverse view: descendingSet()</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>NavigableSet&lt;E&gt; descendingSet()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> a live reversed view</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(1) to create</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Need the same set from biggest to smallest, without building a new one? Ask for the descending view.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> It is a view, so changes through either set show in the other. <code>reversed()</code> (Java 21) gives the same thing.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// descendingSet view\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\nimport java.util.Set;\n\npublic class TreeSetSortDescendingDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(1312, 1356, 1374, 1403));\n\n        System.out.println(\"Ascending  : \" + heights);\n        System.out.println(\"Descending : \" + heights.descendingSet());   // view, no copy\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Ascending  : [1312, 1356, 1374, 1403]\n// Descending : [1403, 1374, 1356, 1312]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>14. Sorted list with positions: new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(treeSet)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>List&lt;E&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(set)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> a sorted list you can index</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> The set has no <code>get(i)</code>. Copy it into a list, which keeps the sorted order, and then ask for the 1st, 2nd or 3rd smallest.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> The copy is O(n) and does not need to sort again. Do it once and reuse the list rather than copying inside a loop.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// sorted set copied to a list\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetSortToListDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(1312, 1356, 1374, 1403));\n\n        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(heights);   // already sorted\n\n        System.out.println(\"Second smallest : \" + list.get(1));\n        System.out.println(\"Third tallest   : \" + list.get(list.size() - 3));\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Second smallest : 1356\n// Third tallest   : 1356"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Which sorting approach to pick?</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;\">\n<tr><th style=\"text-align:left;\">You need</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Pick</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Note</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Small to big or A to Z</td><td><code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;()</code></td><td>Elements must be Comparable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Biggest first</td><td><code>Comparator.reverseOrder()</code></td><td>Set at creation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sort objects by fields</td><td><code>Comparator.comparing(...).thenComparing(...)</code></td><td>End with a unique field</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Case-insensitive strings</td><td><code>String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER</code></td><td>\"Raigad\" equals \"raigad\" here</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Both directions</td><td><code>descendingSet()</code></td><td>View, no copy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Position access</td><td><code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(set)</code></td><td>Copy once</td></tr>\n</table>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Module: Finding (searching) methods</h3>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> a TreeSet can find things a HashSet cannot: \"the smallest\", \"the closest smaller one\", \"everything between 1300 and 1400\". Each card has one tiny program.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> the navigation methods are O(log n) walks down the tree. The views from <code>headSet</code>/<code>tailSet</code>/<code>subSet</code> are O(1) to create, but calling <code>size()</code> on them is O(k) for k elements.</p>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>15. contains(Object o)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>boolean contains(Object o)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> <code>true</code> or <code>false</code></li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Is the value in the set? It walks down the tree, going left or right at each step, so even a million elements need about 20 steps.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Uses the comparator (<code>compareTo</code>), not <code>hashCode</code>. With a case-insensitive comparator, <code>contains(\"RAIGAD\")</code> is true for \"raigad\".</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet contains\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetFindContainsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(1312, 1356, 1374, 1403));\n\n        System.out.println(heights.contains(1356));   // true\n        System.out.println(heights.contains(1500));   // false\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// true\n// false"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>16. first() and last(): the smallest and the biggest</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>E first();  E last()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> the lowest / the highest element</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> No scanning needed: the smallest is at the far left of the tree and the biggest at the far right.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Both throw <code>NoSuchElementException</code> on an empty set. <code>pollFirst</code>/<code>pollLast</code> also remove and return <code>null</code> when empty.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// smallest and biggest in a TreeSet\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetFindFirstLastDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(1312, 1356, 1374, 1403));\n\n        System.out.println(\"Lowest fort  : \" + heights.first());\n        System.out.println(\"Highest fort : \" + heights.last());\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Lowest fort  : 1312\n// Highest fort : 1403"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>17. lower(e) and floor(e): the nearest value below</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>E lower(E e);  E floor(E e)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> the greatest element below (lower) or at-or-below (floor), or <code>null</code></li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> <code>lower</code> means \"strictly smaller\", <code>floor</code> means \"smaller or equal\". Handy for \"what is the last slot before 10:30?\".</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Both return <code>null</code> when nothing qualifies, so check before unboxing an <code>Integer</code> to <code>int</code> (a <code>NullPointerException</code> otherwise).</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// lower and floor\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetFindFloorDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(1312, 1356, 1374, 1403));\n\n        System.out.println(\"lower(1374) : \" + heights.lower(1374));   // 1356: strictly below\n        System.out.println(\"floor(1374) : \" + heights.floor(1374));   // 1374: equal counts\n        System.out.println(\"floor(1000) : \" + heights.floor(1000));   // null: nothing below\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// lower(1374) : 1356\n// floor(1374) : 1374\n// floor(1000) : null"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>18. higher(e) and ceiling(e): the nearest value above</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>E higher(E e);  E ceiling(E e)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> the least element above (higher) or at-or-above (ceiling), or <code>null</code></li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> <code>higher</code> means \"strictly bigger\", <code>ceiling</code> means \"bigger or equal\". Great for \"the next free slot at or after 10:30\".</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Together, <code>floor</code> and <code>ceiling</code> give the two neighbours of any value, which is the basis of \"closest element\" and \"overlap check\" algorithms.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// higher and ceiling\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetFindCeilingDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(1312, 1356, 1374, 1403));\n\n        System.out.println(\"higher(1374)  : \" + heights.higher(1374));    // 1403: strictly above\n        System.out.println(\"ceiling(1374) : \" + heights.ceiling(1374));   // 1374: equal counts\n        System.out.println(\"ceiling(1500) : \" + heights.ceiling(1500));   // null: nothing above\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// higher(1374)  : 1403\n// ceiling(1374) : 1374\n// ceiling(1500) : null"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>19. headSet and tailSet: everything below or above</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>SortedSet&lt;E&gt; headSet(E to);  SortedSet&lt;E&gt; tailSet(E from)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> a live view of the elements below / from a value</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(1) to create</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> <code>headSet(x)</code> is everything before x (x not included). <code>tailSet(x)</code> is x and everything after. Add <code>true</code>/<code>false</code> as a second argument to include or exclude the border.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> These are <strong>views</strong> over the same tree. Adding a value outside the range through the view throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// headSet and tailSet\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetFindHeadTailDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(1312, 1356, 1374, 1403));\n\n        System.out.println(\"Below 1374        : \" + heights.headSet(1374));          // 1374 excluded\n        System.out.println(\"Up to 1374        : \" + heights.headSet(1374, true));    // included\n        System.out.println(\"From 1374 upwards : \" + heights.tailSet(1374));          // 1374 included\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Below 1374        : [1312, 1356]\n// Up to 1374        : [1312, 1356, 1374]\n// From 1374 upwards : [1374, 1403]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>20. subSet: a range between two values</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>NavigableSet&lt;E&gt; subSet(E from, boolean fromInc, E to, boolean toInc)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> a live view of the range</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(1) to create</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Everything between two values, for example all forts between 1300 and 1400 metres. The two booleans say whether the border values are included.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> The short form <code>subSet(from, to)</code> is \"from included, to excluded\". If <code>from</code> is greater than <code>to</code> you get <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// subSet range search\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetFindSubSetDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(1033, 1312, 1356, 1374, 1403, 1424));\n\n        Set<Integer> middle = heights.subSet(1300, true, 1400, true);   // 1300 to 1400, both included\n\n        System.out.println(middle);\n        System.out.println(\"Count in range: \" + middle.size());\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// [1312, 1356, 1374]\n// Count in range: 3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>21. stream().filter(...).findFirst()</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>Optional&lt;E&gt; findFirst()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> the smallest element that follows a rule</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(n), stops early</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> For a rule that is not about the order (for example \"an even number\"), filter the stream. Because the TreeSet is sorted, <code>findFirst</code> gives the <em>smallest</em> match.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> If the rule is about the value itself (at least X), use <code>ceiling(X)</code> instead: it is O(log n), the stream is O(n).</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// smallest match with a stream\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetFindFilterDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> heights = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(1033, 1312, 1356, 1374, 1403));\n\n        Integer firstEven = heights.stream()\n                .filter(h -> h % 2 == 0)   // rule: even height\n                .findFirst()               // smallest, because the set is sorted\n                .orElse(null);\n\n        System.out.println(firstEven);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// 1312"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>22. retainAll(Collection c): values both groups share</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>boolean retainAll(Collection&lt;?&gt; c)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> <code>true</code> if the set changed</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(n log n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Keep only the values that also appear in the other group. The result stays sorted.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> It changes the receiver, so work on a copy: <code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(original)</code>.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// common values, still sorted\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetFindRetainAllDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<String> groupA = new TreeSet<>(List.of(\"Pune\", \"Nashik\", \"Nagpur\", \"Indore\"));\n        List<String> groupB = List.of(\"Surat\", \"Nagpur\", \"Pune\");\n\n        Set<String> common = new TreeSet<>(groupA);   // copy first\n        common.retainAll(groupB);\n\n        System.out.println(common);   // sorted\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// [Nagpur, Pune]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Which finding method to pick?</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;\">\n<tr><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Question</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Method</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Time</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Is this value in the set?</td><td><code>contains</code></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smallest or biggest?</td><td><code>first</code> / <code>last</code></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nearest below (strict / or equal)?</td><td><code>lower</code> / <code>floor</code></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nearest above (strict / or equal)?</td><td><code>higher</code> / <code>ceiling</code></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Everything below / above a value?</td><td><code>headSet</code> / <code>tailSet</code></td><td>O(1) view</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Everything between two values?</td><td><code>subSet</code></td><td>O(1) view</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smallest that follows a rule?</td><td><code>stream().filter().findFirst()</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What do two groups share?</td><td><code>retainAll</code> on a copy</td><td>O(n log n)</td></tr>\n</table>"
    }
